## Proof

Let \(G = L_2(q) = \mathrm{PSL}(2,q)\) with \(q\) odd. We determine the structure of a Sylow \(2\)-subgroup of \(G\).

### Step 1. Order and its \(2\)-part

\[
|G| = \frac{q(q-1)(q+1)}{2}.
\]
Since \(q\) is odd, \(v_2(q)=0\). The two consecutive even integers \(q-1\) and \(q+1\) satisfy: exactly one of them is \(\equiv 2\pmod 4\) (so has \(2\)-adic valuation \(1\)) and the other is \(\equiv 0\pmod 4\) (so has \(2\)-adic valuation \(\ge 2\)). Setting
\[
c := \max\bigl(v_2(q-1),\, v_2(q+1)\bigr) \;\ge\; 2,
\]
we obtain
\[
v_2(|G|) = v_2(q-1)+v_2(q+1)-1 = 1 + c - 1 = c.
\]
Hence every Sylow \(2\)-subgroup of \(G\) has order \(2^c\).

### Step 2. Two dihedral subgroups from the two tori

Work in \(\mathrm{SL}(2,q)\) and project to \(\mathrm{PSL}(2,q)=\mathrm{SL}(2,q)/\{\pm I\}\).

**Split torus.** The diagonal subgroup
\[
T_s = \left\{\begin{pmatrix} t & 0 \\ 0 & t^{-1}\end{pmatrix} : t\in\mathbb F_q^\times\right\}
\]
has order \(q-1\) in \(\mathrm{SL}(2,q)\). Its normalizer is \(T_s\rtimes\langle w\rangle\) with \(w=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}\) (note \(w^2=-I\)), of order \(2(q-1)\). Projecting to \(G\):
\[
|\overline{T}_s| = \frac{q-1}{2},\qquad |N_G(\overline{T}_s)| = q-1,\qquad N_G(\overline{T}_s)/\overline{T}_s\cong C_2.
\]
Thus \(N_G(\overline{T}_s)\) is **dihedral of order \(q-1\)** (rotation subgroup \(\overline{T}_s\) of order \((q-1)/2\)).

**Non-split torus.** Embed \(\mathbb F_{q^2}^\times\) acting on the \(2\)-dimensional \(\mathbb F_q\)-space. The norm-\(1\) subgroup
\[
T_{ns} = \{x\in\mathbb F_{q^2}^\times : N(x)=x\cdot x^q=1\}
\]
has order \(q+1\) in \(\mathrm{SL}(2,q)\). Its normalizer (adjoin the Frobenius involution \(x\mapsto x^q\)) has order \(2(q+1)\). Projecting to \(G\):
\[
|\overline{T}_{ns}|=\frac{q+1}{2},\qquad |N_G(\overline{T}_{ns})|=q+1,\qquad N_G(\overline{T}_{ns})/\overline{T}_{ns}\cong C_2.
\]
Thus \(N_G(\overline{T}_{ns})\) is **dihedral of order \(q+1\)** (rotation subgroup \(\overline{T}_{ns}\) of order \((q+1)/2\)).

### Step 3. A Sylow \(2\)-subgroup sits inside the relevant dihedral normalizer

A Sylow \(2\)-subgroup of a dihedral group \(D_{2m}\) (order \(2m\), rotation of order \(m\)) is itself dihedral: if \(m=2^a r\) with \(r\) odd, the unique cyclic subgroup of order \(2^a\) of the rotation, together with any reflection, generates a dihedral subgroup of order \(2^{a+1}=2\cdot 2^a\), which is the Sylow \(2\)-subgroup.

Now split into two cases.

**Case \(q\equiv 1\pmod 4\).** Then \(v_2(q-1)=c\ge 2\) and \(v_2(q+1)=1\). The dihedral group \(N_G(\overline{T}_s)\) has order \(q-1\), whose \(2\)-part is \(2^c\). Its Sylow \(2\)-subgroup has order \(2^c=v_2(|G|)\), so it is a Sylow \(2\)-subgroup of \(G\), and it is **dihedral of order \(2^c\)**.

**Case \(q\equiv 3\pmod 4\).** Then \(v_2(q+1)=c\ge 2\) and \(v_2(q-1)=1\). The dihedral group \(N_G(\overline{T}_{ns})\) has order \(q+1\), whose \(2\)-part is \(2^c\). Its Sylow \(2\)-subgroup has order \(2^c=v_2(|G|)\), so it is a Sylow \(2\)-subgroup of \(G\), and it is **dihedral of order \(2^c\)**.

### Step 4. Conclusion

In both cases the Sylow \(2\)-subgroup of \(L_2(q)\) is **dihedral of order \(2^c\)**, where
\[
c = \max\bigl(v_2(q-1),\, v_2(q+1)\bigr).
\]

Refining by residue mod \(8\):

- **\(q\equiv \pm 3\pmod 8\)** (i.e. \(q\equiv 3\) or \(5\pmod 8\)): here \(c=2\), so the Sylow \(2\)-subgroup is dihedral of order \(4\), i.e. the **Klein four-group \(V_4\)** (abelian).

- **\(q\equiv \pm 1\pmod 8\)** (i.e. \(q\equiv 1\) or \(7\pmod 8\)): here \(c\ge 3\), so the Sylow \(2\)-subgroup is a **non-abelian dihedral group of order \(2^c\ge 8\)**, namely \(D_{2^{c-1}}\), with \(2^c\) equal to the exact power of \(2\) dividing \(q-1\) (when \(q\equiv 1\pmod 8\)) or \(q+1\) (when \(q\equiv 7\pmod 8\)).

In particular, the Sylow \(2\)-subgroups of \(L_2(q)\) are **always dihedral** (where "dihedral of order \(4\)" is understood as \(V_4\)). This is the classical result of Dickson.

